"""
Problem:
--------
A peak element is an element that is greater than its neighbors.

Return the index of any peak element.

Example:
    nums = [1, 2, 3, 1]

    Output:
        2

    Because nums[2] = 3 is greater than 2 and 1.

Key Idea:
---------
Use Binary Search by checking:

    nums[mid] < nums[mid + 1]

If true:
    We are going uphill.
    A peak must exist on the RIGHT side.

Otherwise:
    We are going downhill.
    `mid` itself could be a peak,
    so keep `mid` in the search range.

Important:
----------
We use:

    right = mid

NOT:

    right = mid - 1

Because `mid` can itself be the peak.

The loop continues while:

    left < right

When:
    left == right

that index must be a peak.

Why does a peak always exist?
-----------------------------
The array is considered to have:

    nums[-1] = -∞
    nums[n]  = -∞

So even if the array keeps increasing or decreasing,
there will always be a peak.

Complexity:
-----------
Time:  O(log n)
Space: O(1)

Memory Trick:
-------------
    nums[mid] < nums[mid+1]
        → Going UP → move RIGHT

    nums[mid] >= nums[mid+1]
        → Going DOWN → keep MID

    left == right
        → Peak found
"""


class Solution:
    def findPeakElement(self, nums):

        # Start of the search range
        left = 0

        # End of the search range
        right = len(nums) - 1

        # Continue until only one index remains
        while left < right:

            # Find the middle index
            mid = (left + right) // 2

            # Check whether we are going uphill
            if nums[mid] < nums[mid + 1]:

                # Going UP → a peak must be on the right
                left = mid + 1

            else:

                # Going DOWN → mid could be the peak
                # Keep mid in the search range
                right = mid

        # left == right → peak index
        return left
